theory_blocks.body:
Some observations are genuinely ambiguous until you dig further. Fizzing (gas bubbles appearing) usually signals a chemical reaction, such as acid reacting with a carbonate to release carbon dioxide — but a bottle of fizzy drink also releases bubbles of carbon dioxide gas that was simply dissolved under pressure, with no new reaction taking place when the cap is opened.

[[blue:The only way to tell these apart is to check whether a new substance has formed]]: in the acid-carbonate case, new substances (a salt, water and carbon dioxide) replace the carbonate and acid that reacted;

in the fizzy drink, the same dissolved carbon dioxide that was already there is simply escaping.

To test the effect of one factor on rate: (1) choose that factor as the **independent variable**, and decide exactly what values to compare (for example, $1.0\ \mathrm{mol/dm^3}$ against $2.0\ \mathrm{mol/dm^3}$ hydrochloric acid);

(2) choose a **dependent variable** to measure — a suitable signal, such as gas volume collected in a syringe every 10 s, or mass lost from the flask every 10 s;

(3) list every other **controlled variable** that could also affect rate (mass and surface area of the solid, volume of acid, temperature, whether a catalyst is present) and keep every one of them identical between the two runs.

A test is only fair, and its result only trustworthy, if exactly one variable differs between the runs being compared. If two variables differ at once, any difference in rate cannot be attributed to either one specifically.

