theory_blocks.body:
In terms of oxygen: **oxidation** is the gain of oxygen, and **reduction** is the loss of oxygen. In the reaction $\mathrm{CuO + H_{2} \rightarrow Cu + H_{2}O}$, copper(II) oxide loses its oxygen to become copper metal, so copper(II) oxide is **reduced**;

hydrogen gains that same oxygen to become water, so hydrogen is **oxidised**. The oxygen that disappears from one substance is exactly the oxygen that appears on the other — nothing is created or destroyed,

only transferred, which is why one substance's reduction always comes with another's oxidation in the same equation.

A more general definition covers every redox reaction, including ones with no oxygen anywhere: **oxidation** is the loss of electrons, and **reduction** is the gain of electrons, remembered as **OIL RIG** (Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain).

In the reaction $\mathrm{Zn + Cu^{2+} \rightarrow Zn^{2+} + Cu}$, zinc atoms each lose two electrons to become $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ ions, so zinc is **oxidised**;

copper(II) ions each gain those same two electrons to become copper atoms, so copper(II) is **reduced**.

This can be written as two half-equations that add up to the full equation: $\mathrm{Zn \rightarrow Zn^{2+} + 2e^-}$ (oxidation) and $\mathrm{Cu^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow Cu}$ (reduction);

the electrons lost in the first exactly match the electrons gained in the second.

The figure below places an oxygen-transfer example and an electron-transfer example side by side: in both, notice that whatever one substance loses, the other gains, in the same reaction.

